CAT = "gulfrnFund"; SEG = "FUN"
Q = [
("What is the most effective single measure to prevent the spread of infection in hospital?",
 "Hand hygiene", ["Wearing a mask", "Daily linen changes", "Antibiotic prophylaxis"],
 "Hand hygiene at the right moments is the single most effective way to cut healthcare-associated infection. Masks and linen changes help only with specific routes."),
("How long should a nurse rub hands with an alcohol-based hand rub?",
 "20 to 30 seconds", ["5 to 10 seconds", "40 to 60 seconds", "2 to 3 minutes"],
 "WHO guidance is 20 to 30 seconds for an alcohol rub, until the hands are dry. Washing with soap and water takes 40 to 60 seconds."),
("When must a nurse wash hands with soap and water rather than use an alcohol rub?",
 "When hands are visibly dirty", ["Before any aseptic procedure", "After touching the patient's bed", "Before taking a radial pulse"],
 "Alcohol rub does not remove visible dirt or body fluid, so soiled hands need soap and water. Soap and water is also preferred after caring for a patient with C. difficile diarrhoea."),
("Which is the first step of the nursing process?",
 "Assessment", ["Diagnosis", "Planning", "Evaluation"],
 "The nursing process runs assessment, diagnosis, planning, implementation and evaluation. Every later step depends on the data collected during assessment."),
("Which piece of data is objective?",
 "Blood pressure 150/94 mmHg", ["Patient reports a headache", "Patient says she feels dizzy", "Patient describes nausea"],
 "Objective data are measured or observed by the nurse, such as a blood pressure reading. Symptoms the patient reports are subjective."),
("Which is the correct order for examining the abdomen?",
 "Inspect, auscultate, percuss, palpate", ["Inspect, palpate, percuss, auscultate", "Palpate, percuss, auscultate, inspect", "Auscultate, inspect, palpate, percuss"],
 "Bowel sounds are heard before percussion and palpation because touching the abdomen can change them. Other body systems use inspect, palpate, percuss, auscultate."),
("What is a normal adult resting respiratory rate?",
 "12 to 20 breaths per minute", ["6 to 10 breaths per minute", "22 to 28 breaths per minute", "30 to 40 breaths per minute"],
 "A resting adult usually breathes 12 to 20 times a minute. Thirty to forty a minute is normal for an infant, not an adult."),
("A patient's temperature is 38.4°C. How is this best described?",
 "Fever", ["Normal", "Hypothermia", "Hyperpyrexia"],
 "Most references treat a core temperature of 38°C or above as fever. Hyperpyrexia is a very high fever, usually above about 41°C."),
("Which site gives the closest reading to core temperature in routine ward care?",
 "Tympanic", ["Axillary", "Oral after a hot drink", "Skin of the forehead"],
 "A correctly placed tympanic probe reflects core temperature well. Axillary and skin readings run lower, and a hot drink falsely raises an oral reading for about 15 to 30 minutes."),
("A patient is at high risk of pressure injury. How often should the nurse at least reposition them in bed?",
 "Every 2 hours", ["Every 6 hours", "Once per shift", "Only when they ask"],
 "Two-hourly repositioning is the classic minimum for an immobile, high-risk patient, adjusted to skin checks and the support surface in use."),
("A stage 2 pressure injury is best described as:",
 "Partial-thickness skin loss", ["Intact skin with non-blanching redness", "Full-thickness loss with exposed bone", "Full-thickness loss with visible fat"],
 "Stage 2 is partial-thickness loss of the dermis, often a shallow open area or a blister. Non-blanching redness on intact skin is stage 1; visible fat is stage 3 and exposed bone stage 4."),
("Which tool is used to assess a patient's risk of pressure injury?",
 "Braden scale", ["Glasgow Coma Scale", "Morse scale", "Apgar score"],
 "The Braden scale scores sensory perception, moisture, activity, mobility, nutrition and friction. The Morse scale is for fall risk and the Glasgow Coma Scale for consciousness."),
("A patient on bed rest develops a red, warm, swollen calf. What should the nurse do first?",
 "Keep the leg still and report it", ["Massage the calf firmly", "Walk the patient in the corridor", "Apply a hot water bottle"],
 "These are signs of deep vein thrombosis. Massaging or exercising the leg could dislodge the clot, so the nurse keeps the limb at rest and informs the doctor promptly."),
("Which position is best for a patient with severe shortness of breath?",
 "High Fowler's", ["Supine", "Trendelenburg", "Prone"],
 "Sitting upright lets the diaphragm descend and the chest expand fully. Lying flat or head-down pushes the abdominal contents against the diaphragm."),
("Before inserting a nasogastric tube, how is the length to insert measured?",
 "Nose to earlobe to xiphoid", ["Mouth to chin to sternal notch", "Nose to umbilicus", "Earlobe to nipple line"],
 "The tube is measured from the tip of the nose to the earlobe and then down to the xiphoid process, which estimates the distance to the stomach."),
("What is the most reliable bedside check that a new nasogastric tube is in the stomach?",
 "Testing the aspirate for an acid pH", ["Bubbles when the end is in water", "Air whooshed in with a syringe", "Patient can still speak"],
 "An acidic aspirate supports gastric placement; the cut-off is set by local policy (often 5.5 or below, some guidelines 5 or below). An X-ray is the definitive check and is used when no acid aspirate is obtained. The whoosh test is no longer considered safe."),
("A patient is having a tube feed. What position should be maintained during the feed?",
 "Head up 30 to 45 degrees", ["Lying flat on the back", "Head lower than the feet", "Lying on the left side"],
 "Keeping the head of the bed raised at least 30 degrees during feeding and for a while after lowers the risk of aspiration."),
("How is a 24-hour urine collection carried out?",
 "Discard the first void, then collect all", ["Keep the first void and stop at 12 hours", "Collect only the morning samples", "Collect every second void"],
 "The test starts with an empty bladder: the first void is discarded and the start time noted, then every void for 24 hours is kept, including one at the end time."),
("What is the minimum acceptable urine output for an adult?",
 "0.5 mL/kg per hour", ["0.1 mL/kg per hour", "2 mL/kg per hour", "5 mL/kg per hour"],
 "An adult output below about 0.5 mL/kg per hour, roughly 30 mL an hour for a 60 kg adult, suggests poor renal perfusion and should be reported."),
("Which technique is used when inserting an indwelling urinary catheter?",
 "Sterile technique", ["Clean technique", "No-touch with bare hands", "Medical asepsis only"],
 "Catheterisation enters a sterile body cavity, so it is done with sterile gloves and equipment to prevent catheter-associated urinary tract infection."),
("An adult patient falls. What should the nurse do first?",
 "Check for injury first", ["Help them straight back to bed", "Complete the incident report", "Call the family to inform them"],
 "The patient is checked for head injury, fractures and consciousness before being moved, so that moving them does not worsen an injury. The report is written afterwards."),
("Which is the safest use of bed rails for a confused patient?",
 "Follow a risk assessment", ["Raise all four rails always", "Use a waist restraint instead", "Tie the patient's wrists"],
 "Bed rails can cause entrapment and worse falls when a confused patient climbs over them. Use is decided by an individual risk assessment and a low bed is often safer."),
("A conscious patient refuses a prescribed treatment after a full explanation. What should the nurse do?",
 "Respect it and tell the doctor", ["Give it while they are asleep", "Ask the family to sign for them", "Hide it in their food"],
 "A competent adult may refuse treatment. The nurse makes sure the patient understands the risks, documents the refusal and informs the prescriber."),
("Which diet is usually ordered first after the return of bowel sounds following surgery?",
 "Clear fluids", ["Regular diet", "High-fibre diet", "Soft diet with meat"],
 "Feeding usually progresses from clear fluids to full fluids, then soft and normal diet, as tolerated. Many units now advance faster under enhanced recovery plans."),
("A patient has a BMI of 31 kg/m². How is this classified?",
 "Obese", ["Normal weight", "Overweight", "Underweight"],
 "WHO classes a BMI of 30 or more as obese, 25 to 29.9 as overweight and 18.5 to 24.9 as normal."),
("Which nutrient is the body's main source of energy?",
 "Carbohydrate", ["Protein", "Vitamin C", "Dietary fibre"],
 "Carbohydrate supplies most of the body's energy. Protein is mainly used for building and repairing tissue, and vitamins and minerals give no energy."),
("Which is the most important check before giving a blood transfusion?",
 "Patient identity at the bedside", ["The colour of the blood in the bag", "The time of the patient's last meal", "The patient's weight"],
 "Most fatal transfusion reactions come from giving the wrong blood to the wrong patient. Two staff check the patient's identity against the unit at the bedside."),
("During a blood transfusion the patient develops chills, back pain and fever. What should the nurse do first?",
 "Stop the transfusion", ["Slow the rate by half", "Give paracetamol and continue", "Flush the line with dextrose"],
 "These are signs of an acute haemolytic reaction. The transfusion is stopped at once, the line kept open with normal saline through new tubing, and the doctor and blood bank informed."),
]
